112: % Saturn's moon \titan{} is unique amongst solar system bodies in simultaneously
113: % possessing a dense nitrogen-dominated atmosphere, a global methane--ethane
114: % precipitation and lake cycle that is structurally analogous to the terrestrial
115: % hydrological cycle, the largest inventory of complex organic chemistry in the
116: % solar system outside Earth, confirmed polar hydrocarbon seas containing an
117: % estimated $\sim$70{,}000\,km$^3$ of liquid methane--ethane--nitrogen, and a
118: % deep interior water--ammonia ocean inferred from tidal deformation
119: % measurements.  This convergence of chemical feedstock, cycling solvent, and
120: % liquid environments makes \titan{} not merely a potentially habitable world
121: % but a natural laboratory for prebiotic chemistry at planetary scale.
122: 
123: % This thesis presents a quantitative, spatially-resolved probabilistic
124: % framework for assessing \titan{}'s surface and near-surface habitability at
125: % five temporal configurations spanning the Late Heavy Bombardment
126: % ($-3.5\gya$), a lake-formation transition epoch ($-1.0\gya$), the
127: % present-day \cassini{} epoch, a near-future solar warming window ($+0.25\gya$),
128: % and the red-giant future ($+5.9\gya$).  Eight observational
129: % habitability-proxy features are derived from the complete \cassini{} dataset
130: % --- synthetic aperture radar (SAR), near-infrared spectroscopy (VIMS),
131: % thermal mapping (CIRS), radar altimetry, and the Lopes et al.\ (2019) global
132: % geomorphological map --- and combined through a Bayesian Beta-conjugate
133: % posterior update to yield probability maps $P(\text{habitable}\mid\featurevec)$
134: % at \SI{4490}{\metre\per\text{px}} resolution across the full globe.
135: 
136: % At the present epoch, habitability is highest along the margins of the north
137: % polar hydrocarbon seas, where posterior means reach $P(H)\approx0.55$--0.57.
138: % During the Late Heavy Bombardment, impact-melt ponds at major crater sites
139: % constituted the dominant habitability zones.  In the far future, solar
140: % red-giant expansion will produce a near-globally habitable water--ammonia
141: % ocean over an extraordinary accumulated organic substrate.
142: 
143: % The analysis independently validates the NASA \dragonfly{} mission's selection
144: % of Selk crater: although its present-day solvent habitability is modest
145: % (the $\sim$6th percentile of present-epoch and $\sim$40th percentile of
146: % past-epoch habitability), its geomorphological diversity is the highest of any
147: % equatorial site ($6.8\times$ the global median) --- the Cassini proxy for the
148: % past water--organic chemistry that \dragonfly{} targets.  The instrument suite
149: % is shown to be well-matched to the dominant habitability drivers at the target site.
150: 
