1: \section{Dependent Types via Singletons}
2: When moving from STLC to dependent types, our judgements themselves become more complicated.
3: To keep things simple, we'll only consider the simplest possible dependent type: the singleton.
4: In order to make the calculus non-trivial, we'll include the booleans as a base type.
5: 
6: \subsection{Presyntax}
7: Our calculus is defined over precontexts $\Gamma$, pretypes $A$, and preterms $t, c, e$.
8: 
9: \begin{bnf}
10: $\Gamma$ ::=
11:   | $\cdot$ : Empty context
12:   | $\Gamma, x \colon S$ : Variable weakening
13:   | $\Gamma, (x \colon A) \equiv t$ : Definition weakening
14: ;;
15: $A$ ::=
16:   | $\Bool$ : Booleans
17:   | $\Singleton[A][t]$ : The singleton of $t \,\colon A$
18: ;;
19: $t, c, e$ ::=
20: | $\true$ : Boolean value constructors
21: | $\false$
22: | $\when{c}{x}{A}{t}{e}$ : Boolean eliminator
23: | $\val[A][t]$ : Value constructor for $\Singleton[A][t]$
24: | $x, y, z, ...$ : Variables
25: \end{bnf}
26: 
27: Intuition for the boolean eliminator is that it acts like if-then-else, except it also binds the value of $c$ to $x$, and makes this available for $A$ to use.
